# runtime/abi/lang_items.def — canonical lang-item symbol registry
#
# The single source of truth for every `__cryo_`-prefixed runtime symbol the
# compiler emits calls into. A symbol's spelling lives here and nowhere else in
# prose: runtime code defines each item as a `![no_mangle]` function whose
# source name IS the symbol, and generated code references it by codegen, never
# by a hand-typed string. When adding or changing a lang item, edit this file
# first, then the definition.
#
# Columns: SYMBOL | SIGNATURE | DEFINED-IN | STATUS
#
# DEFINED-IN is what `cryogate abi` (`make runtime-abi-check`) checks against the built
# archives, so it is a list, not prose: the link KINDS whose runtime must
# define the symbol - `freestanding`, `hosted`, either narrowed to one OS as
# `freestanding:linux` - and `hosted` means both hosted configurations, abort
# and unwind. `import` is a system symbol the runtime calls and never defines;
# `compiler` is a name the compiler gives a symbol of its own, listed so that
# every `__cryo_` spelling in compiler/src is accounted for here (a trailing
# `*` makes it a prefix). The check also refuses an exported `__cryo_` symbol
# no row names, and a `__cryo_` call in compiler/src or stdlib/ no row names.
#
# ABI conventions (settled; this file is their source of truth):
#   - string   : the primitive thin, NUL-terminated `i8*` (8 bytes). This is the
#                panic message/file operand type. No compiler change: today's
#                `@panic` already passes `i8*`, and message/file are always
#                codegen-emitted static NUL-terminated literals, so the call
#                sites build no fat pointer. (No embedded NUL / explicit length —
#                fine for static panic strings.)
#   - Location : POD { file: string, line: u32, column: u32, function: string },
#                24 bytes (see layout.def), the layout of the language's
#                `std::core::intrinsics::Location`, passed BY POINTER. A check
#                site's is a private constant made from compile-time data, so it
#                is still zero-alloc; by-pointer avoids classifying the aggregate
#                by hand under two different C ABIs (SysV vs Win64).
#   - File and function strings are static, codegen-emitted bytes — never heap.
#   - The panic seam is abort-now / unwind-ready: the check helpers CALL
#     __cryo_panic; nothing inlines abort/trap assuming the abort strategy.
#     Adding `--panic=unwind` later must require no change to any core/ file.

# --- Entry (freestanding profile) ------------------------------------------
# Per-OS. AMD and Intel share the x86-64 ISA -> one x86-64 codepath covers both
# vendors; the split that matters is OS ABI (Linux vs Windows). Present only in
# no_runtime builds; a hosted program starts at the hosted entry below.
#
# Linux: the ELF entry the linker resolves under `-nostartfiles`. Reads argc/argv
# off the initial stack, hands them to __cryo_start. Fully freestanding (no libc,
# no imports). Exit = `exit_group` syscall.
_start          | ![naked] () -> void                       | freestanding:linux | DONE
# Startup trampoline: runs the user `main` and exits with its return. `main` is
# an external C-ABI symbol resolved from the user object.
__cryo_start    | (argc: i64, argv: i8**) -> never          | freestanding | DONE (dual-OS)
#
# Windows: PE startup passes no stack args and has no stable raw-syscall ABI, so
# the entry links ntdll (its sole dependency) and exits via NtTerminateProcess.
# Because the loader passes nothing on the stack, arguments are read from the PEB
# instead: ProcessParameters.CommandLine is one UTF-16 UNICODE_STRING, so the
# entry owns both the split (the CRT's documented quoting rules) and the
# UTF-16 -> UTF-8 transcode (a surrogate with no partner becomes U+FFFD). The
# process heap (also from the PEB) sizes the argv vector, so nothing is capped
# or truncated, and the vector ends in a null slot. It is the only split of the
# command line: a hosted `main` receives this vector and std::env::args() reads
# it back, so no shell32 CommandLineToArgvW and no second set of rules.
# The driver's no_runtime link emits
# `-nostartfiles -nostdlib -Wl,-e,_start -lntdll` for a windows target.
_start          | ![naked] () -> void  (PE entry, /entry=_start) | freestanding:windows | DONE
# ntdll import, called by the windows exit path with (HANDLE)-1.
NtTerminateProcess | (process: i64, status: i32) -> i32  (extern, ntdll) | import | (system)
# Satisfies the mingw backend's `call __main` in main's prologue (C global-ctor
# bootstrap). Empty in a freestanding build because there is no CRT and Cryo
# emits no constructors, so there is genuinely nothing to run; a hosted build
# does NOT define this -- it calls mingw's real __main instead.
__main          | () -> void                                | freestanding:windows | DONE
#
# __cryo_start is shared (both OSes): capture arguments, run `main`, exit with
# its return. Capture itself is per-OS, because the two OSes hand arguments over
# by different mechanisms: Linux's _start stages the kernel-supplied argc/argv
# into the SysV arg registers (rdi/rsi) and capture only records them; Windows
# receives nothing on the stack (rcx/rdx are zeroed) and parses the PEB command
# line instead. Programs read the captured values through these accessors, which
# report 0 / null if the OS could not supply arguments.
__cryo_argc     | () -> i64                                 | freestanding | DONE (dual-OS)
__cryo_argv     | () -> i8**                                | freestanding | DONE (dual-OS)

# --- Entry (hosted profile) -------------------------------------------------
# A hosted LINUX program starts where glibc's does: crt1's `_start` calls
# __libc_start_main, which runs .preinit_array/_init/.init_array once, registers
# the dynamic linker's finalizer and the executable's .fini_array, and calls the
# widened `main`, exiting through libc `exit`. The driver passes no `-e` there,
# and the runtime defines no hosted Linux entry.
#
# A hosted WINDOWS program starts in Cryo, so that its arguments come from the
# runtime's one command-line parser. The link KEEPS the crt objects and only
# retargets the entry symbol (`-Wl,-e,__cryo_entry`); it does NOT pass
# -nostartfiles. Dropping them takes atexit + _pei386_runtime_relocator with
# them, and each of those is a LINK failure -- `atexit` unconditionally so,
# because libmingwex references it. `-e` also EXTRACTS the entry from the
# runtime archive: ld enters the entry symbol as an undefined reference.
#
# Entering past mingw's startup means owning what it would have driven: call
# _pei386_runtime_relocator (fixes auto-imported DLL *data*; msvcrt's _environ
# reads as garbage without it) then mingw's real __main (C global ctors,
# idempotent). Skipping either leaves every static constructor unrun.
#
# The entry is NOT `![naked]`: the loader `call`s it on an ordinary frame -- and
# a real frame carries the .pdata unwind info a naked stub lacks.
#
# A `--release-static` link keeps the toolchain's own entry.
__cryo_entry        | () -> never                            | hosted:windows | DONE
# Startup trampoline: image fixups, capture arguments, run static constructors,
# then `main` -- which here carries the WIDENED C signature
# `int main(int, char**, char**)` codegen emits for a hosted program, not the
# zero-arg freestanding form. Terminates through libc `exit`, which is what
# flushes buffered stdio and runs atexit handlers.
__cryo_hosted_start | (argc: i64, argv: i8**) -> never       | hosted:windows | DONE
# A startup service the retained crt objects provide, called BY the hosted entry
# rather than defined by it (mingw crt2.o).
_pei386_runtime_relocator | () -> void  (extern, crt2.o)     | import | (system)

# --- Link line -------------------------------------------------------------
# The runtime is one project (runtime/lib.cryo), built per CONFIGURATION into one
# archive each: freestanding (`--no-runtime`, which aborts), hosted under abort,
# hosted under unwind. Its declarations are gated on the build's own options
# (`no_runtime`, `panic_unwind`), so an archive defines exactly the symbols of
# its configuration. The compiler that links a program builds that program's
# configuration on first use (compiler/src/compiler/prebuilt_runtime.cryo),
# from the runtime source it finds (CRYO_RUNTIME, then CRYO_HOME/runtime, then
# beside the compiler binary, then beside the stdlib), into
# `<runtime>/.bin/<triple>/<configuration>/<key>/libcryort.a`.
#
# A link names that one archive: append_freestanding_runtime_libs follows it
# with the system libraries it imports (kernel32/ntdll) on Windows, and
# append_hosted_runtime_libs follows it with (windows only)
# `-Wl,-e,__cryo_entry`. The windows `extra_system_libs` (-lntdll) already
# follows on both link paths, which the entry needs for the PEB argv parse.

# --- Panic funnel ----------------------------------------------------------
# The single divergent sink: print "panicked at <file>:<line>:<column> in
# <function>: <msg>" to stderr (no column when it is 0, no function when it has
# no name), then terminate (exit code 101). panic/abort.cryo DEFINES it in the
# freestanding and hosted-abort configurations, dual-OS; panic/unwind.cryo
# defines the same symbol to unwind instead in the hosted-unwind one.
#
# Freestanding, abort writes with Linux write(2) / Windows WriteFile and exits
# via exit_group / NtTerminateProcess. Hosted it keeps the same write path but
# exits through libc `exit`, which runs atexit handlers and flushes stdio — a raw
# exit there would silently discard whatever the panicking program had buffered.
# The divergence lives entirely in CryoRt::Sys; the funnel itself is identical.
#
# Codegen is routed here: `emit_panic_call` emits a call to the external
# `__cryo_panic` for every panic, and the link names the one runtime archive
# of the build's configuration.
__cryo_panic              | (msg: string, loc: Location*) -> never           | freestanding, hosted | DONE (both strategies, dual-OS; compiler routed)

# --- Unwind surface ---------------------------------------------------------
# BOTH panic strategies export every symbol below, and differ only in
# behaviour. The abort strategy's copies are reachable-but-inert stubs, present
# because a program's objects can name a personality or the caught-panic
# accessors under abort too. Dropping them from either strategy turns a
# strategy switch into an undefined-symbol link error.
#
# `__cryo_personality_v0` is the one lang item whose SIGNATURE is per-OS. Linux
# names it as the LLVM `personality` and the SysV unwinder calls it with the
# Itanium 5-argument form. Windows-gnu emits the same symbol into `.xdata` as the
# frame's language handler, so the OS calls it with the 4-argument SEH form; the
# unwind strategy bridges that to the shared Itanium logic via mingw's
# `_GCC_specific_handler`, which is how libstdc++'s `__gxx_personality_seh0`
# works. Declaring the Itanium form on Windows would read garbage arguments.
# Emitted by codegen (`ExprOps::get_or_decl_personality`), not hand-typed.
__cryo_personality_v0     | per-OS: Itanium (linux) / SEH (windows)           | freestanding, hosted | DONE (dual-OS; SEH-backed on windows)
__cryo_panic_finish       | () -> never                                        | freestanding, hosted | DONE (root-catch tail, emitted under unwind)
__cryo_panic_taken_message| () -> string                                       | freestanding, hosted | DONE (abort's returns null - unreachable there)
__cryo_panic_taken_file   | () -> string                                       | freestanding, hosted | DONE (abort's returns null - unreachable there)
__cryo_panic_taken_line   | () -> u32                                          | freestanding, hosted | DONE (abort's returns 0 - unreachable there)
# Writes the report of the panic this thread last caught without ending the
# process: std::thread's body runner calls it when it catches the body's panic,
# so a panicked thread reports itself before its joiner reads the payload.
__cryo_panic_write_report | () -> void                                         | freestanding, hosted | DONE (abort's writes nothing - unreachable there)

# --- Check handlers (route raw operands through the funnel) -----------------
# Each takes RAW operands (not a pre-formatted string) plus a Location POD
# ({file,line,column,function}, 24 bytes; see layout.def), formats its own
# message into a stack buffer, and diverges via __cryo_panic. DEFINED by
# both panic strategies, dual-OS.
# The compiler emits a call to one of these at every check site in every
# configuration (ExprOps::emit_check_failure), so a failed check reports the
# same message under abort, unwind and freestanding; under unwind the call is
# an `invoke` when the frame has locals to drop. `op` is a raw arithmetic-op
# code; 2 = multiply (the array-size guard) is the only value emitted so far.
__cryo_panic_bounds_check | (loc: Location*, index: u64, len: u64) -> never  | freestanding, hosted | DONE (runtime def + compiler emit, dual-OS)
__cryo_panic_overflow     | (loc: Location*, op: u32, lhs: u64, rhs: u64) -> never | freestanding, hosted | DONE (runtime def + compiler emit, dual-OS)
__cryo_panic_div_zero     | (loc: Location*) -> never                        | freestanding, hosted | DONE (runtime def + compiler emit, dual-OS)
__cryo_panic_no_match     | (loc: Location*) -> never                        | freestanding, hosted | DONE (runtime def + compiler emit, dual-OS)

# --- Allocator (weak / user-overridable) -----------------------------------
# Every heap site routes through ExprOps::emit_raw_alloc / emit_raw_dealloc
# rather than an open-coded copy each. The resolver ladder is
# arena / allocator::alloc / __cryo_alloc / malloc, and the lang-item step fires
# under `no_runtime`.
#
# Freestanding that step is not an optimisation, it is the only option:
# `-nostdlib` leaves no libc `malloc` and `no_std` leaves no stdlib allocator,
# so before it existed EVERY `new` in a freestanding program failed codegen with
# "'malloc' not declared" -- the language's own heap syntax was unusable.
#
# A hosted build keeps the malloc-backed ladder. Repointing it too would make
# every hosted allocation an mmap/VirtualAlloc (the allocator maps once PER
# ALLOCATION), so a hosted repoint waits on a segment/free-list allocator behind
# the same symbol.
#
# `size` is passed 0 at `delete` sites: the site does not carry the allocation
# size, and the allocator recovers the exact mapping from its own header instead.
__cryo_alloc    | (size: u64, align: u64) -> void*          | freestanding | DONE (runtime def weak dual-OS + compiler funnel, no_runtime)
__cryo_dealloc  | (ptr: void*, size: u64, align: u64) -> void | freestanding | DONE (runtime def weak dual-OS + compiler funnel, no_runtime)

# --- Backtrace / diagnostics -----------------------------------------------
# Print one hex return address per stack frame to stderr. Raw addresses only;
# symbolization (address -> name via ELF/DWARF) is a follow-up. The WALK is
# per-OS because the two ABIs record frames differently: Linux follows the rbp
# chain (frame_address(0) + saved-rbp links, terminated by _start's zeroed rbp),
# while Win64 unwinding is table-based, so codegen omits the rbp chain entirely
# and there is nothing to follow -- Windows walks the .pdata RUNTIME_FUNCTION
# tables with a bounded RtlVirtualUnwind loop instead.
#
# ntdll RtlCaptureStackBackTrace was the intended Windows mechanism and is NOT
# usable from a freestanding image: called from a `-nostartfiles -nostdlib` PE it
# runs away and exhausts the whole 2 MB stack. The TEB is NOT the problem -- it
# is present with valid StackBase/StackLimit, and adding .seh_proc unwind info to
# _start does not change the outcome. The image carries no language exception
# handler, so a fault inside the unwinder cannot be dispatched. Hence the
# self-bounded walk. Do not re-try RtlCaptureStackBackTrace.
__cryo_backtrace | () -> void                                | freestanding | DONE (dual-OS: linux rbp chain, windows bounded RtlVirtualUnwind over .pdata)

# --- Memory functions and stack probe (freestanding) -----------------------
# The backend lowers a large copy, a fill or an aggregate compare to calls to
# these C names, and on Windows a frame of a page or more to a ___chkstk_ms
# call, whatever the program wrote. A hosted link takes them from the C
# library and libgcc; a freestanding link has neither, so the runtime defines
# them (weak, string-instruction asm bodies), and `cryogate freestanding`
# runs a program that calls each.
memcpy          | (destination: u8*, source: u8*, count: u64) -> u8* | freestanding | DONE (weak, dual-OS)
memmove         | (destination: u8*, source: u8*, count: u64) -> u8* | freestanding | DONE (weak, dual-OS)
memset          | (destination: u8*, value: i32, count: u64) -> u8*  | freestanding | DONE (weak, dual-OS)
memcmp          | (left: u8*, right: u8*, count: u64) -> i32         | freestanding | DONE (weak, dual-OS)
___chkstk_ms    | ![naked] probe of `rax` bytes, every register kept | freestanding:windows | DONE

# --- Names the compiler gives its own symbols --------------------------------
# Not runtime symbols: each is defined in the program's own objects. Listed so
# that a `__cryo_` call newly emitted by codegen cannot go unregistered.
__cryo_user_main         | the user's `main` under test-main codegen   | compiler | (compiler)
__cryo_test_main         | the generated test runner entry             | compiler | (compiler)
__cryo_test_descriptors  | the generated test table                    | compiler | (compiler)
__cryo_test_name_*       | one test's name constant                    | compiler | (compiler)
__cryo_macro_probe*      | bindgen's macro-evaluation probes           | compiler | (compiler)
__cryo_try_*             | `?` desugaring temporaries                  | compiler | (compiler)
__cryo_for_iter_*        | `for` desugaring temporaries                | compiler | (compiler)
__cryo_coalesce_*        | `??` desugaring temporaries                 | compiler | (compiler)
